# External Frameworks for Enterprise AI Governance
## SECTION A — Strategic AI Use-Case Scoping
### (Pass 1 of 3 — company-agnostic external research)

> **How to read this document.** This is the first of three deep-dive passes (A = scoping, B = measurement, C = risk/appropriate-use). Every named framework below is given the same eight-part treatment: **(1) Origin** — who/when/which publication; **(2) Mechanism** — how it actually works, step by step; **(3) Exact scales/inputs/formula** — the precise numbers, not a summary; **(4) Primary-source anchor** — verbatim clause/subcategory text where it matters; **(5) Worked example** — applied to a realistic enterprise AI use case; **(6) AI-specific adaptation** — how it changes for generative-AI work; **(7) Limitations & failure modes**; **(8) Selection guidance** — when to pick it over rivals. Nothing here is tailored to any specific company; adaptation is a later step.
>
> Section A covers four leaves: **A1 Intake Forms · A2 Scoping KPIs · A3 Prioritization Criteria · A4 Lifecycle Governance Process.**

---

# LEAF A1 — INTAKE FORMS

**What this leaf must accomplish.** An AI use-case intake form is the *front door control*. It does four jobs simultaneously: (a) it forces a proposer to articulate the problem before a solution; (b) it captures exactly the information a reviewer needs to triage, with nothing extraneous; (c) it generates the first audit artifact in the governance record; and (d) it routes the request to the right risk tier. The frameworks below supply the field taxonomy and the process mechanics.

---

## A1·Framework 1 — NIST AI RMF "MAP" Function (the scoping backbone)

**(1) Origin.** NIST AI Risk Management Framework 1.0 (NIST AI 100-1), published January 26, 2023, by the U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology under the National AI Initiative Act of 2020. The companion **AI RMF Playbook** (first complete version March 30, 2023, maintained as a living web resource at airc.nist.gov) supplies subcategory-level suggested actions, documentation prompts, and references. The framework is voluntary, rights-preserving, non-sector-specific, and use-case-agnostic.

**(2) Mechanism.** The AI RMF Core has four functions — **GOVERN, MAP, MEASURE, MANAGE**. MAP is explicitly the *context-and-scoping* function. NIST's own description: MAP "establishes the context to frame risks related to an AI system," and the stated end-state is decisive for intake design — *"After completing the map function, Framework users should have sufficient contextual knowledge about AI system impacts to inform an initial go/no-go decision about whether to design, develop, or deploy an AI system."* That sentence is the entire justification for a structured intake form: the form **is** the data-capture instrument for MAP, and a completed form **is** the evidence supporting the go/no-go gate. MAP has 5 categories and 18 subcategories; the intake-relevant ones map almost one-to-one onto form fields.

**(3) Exact subcategories → intake fields.** The mapping (subcategory text is verbatim from NIST AI 100-1, Table 2):

| MAP subcategory (verbatim) | Intake field it generates |
|---|---|
| **Map 1.1** — "Intended purposes, potentially beneficial uses, context-specific laws, norms and expectations, and prospective settings in which the AI system will be deployed are understood and documented." | Problem statement; intended purpose; deployment context; applicable laws/norms |
| **Map 1.3** — "The organization's mission and relevant goals for AI technology are understood and documented." | Strategic-alignment field (which objective this serves) |
| **Map 1.4** — "The business value or context of business use has been clearly defined or — in the case of assessing existing AI systems — re-evaluated." | Expected value / business case |
| **Map 1.5** — "Organizational risk tolerances are determined and documented." | Risk-tier flag against stated tolerance |
| **Map 1.6** — "System requirements (e.g., 'the system shall respect the privacy of its users') are elicited from and understood by relevant AI actors." | Requirements & constraints; data-privacy requirement |
| **Map 2.1** — "The specific tasks and methods used to implement the tasks that the AI system will support are defined (e.g., classifiers, generative models, recommenders)." | Proposed AI solution / capability type |
| **Map 2.2** — "Information about the AI system's knowledge limits and how system output may be utilized and overseen by humans is documented." | Human-oversight model; where a human stays in the loop |
| **Map 2.3** — "Scientific integrity and TEVV considerations are identified and documented, including those related to … data collection and selection (e.g., availability, representativeness, suitability)." | Data needed; data availability/representativeness (the data-readiness field) |
| **Map 3.1** — "Potential benefits of intended AI system functionality and performance are examined and documented." | Expected benefits / success metrics |
| **Map 5.1** (impact assessment) | Affected individuals/communities; impact flags |

**(4) Primary-source anchor.** Beyond the table above, the Playbook's GOVERN guidance instructs organizations to *"Establish impact assessment policies and processes for AI systems used by the organization"* and to be able to answer documentation prompts such as *"How has the entity documented the AI system's data provenance, including sources, origins, transformations, augmentations, labels, dependencies, constraints, and metadata?"* — a direct specification of intake data-lineage fields.

**(5) Worked example.** Intake for *"GenAI assistant that drafts vendor-negotiation letters for the merchandising team."* MAP 1.1 → purpose: reduce drafting time; context: internal, B2B vendor comms. MAP 2.1 → method: generative LLM with retrieval over prior letters. MAP 2.2 → oversight: buyer reviews and signs every letter (human-in-the-loop, no autosend). MAP 2.3 → data: historical letters (available, representative) but contains pricing terms (sensitive). MAP 1.4 → value: ~6 buyer-hours/week. The completed MAP record is exactly the intake form, and it surfaces the one real risk (pricing-term leakage) at the front door.

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** MAP is *already* AI-specific — it was written for AI systems. For generative AI specifically, pair it with the **NIST Generative AI Profile (NIST AI 600-1, July 2024)**, which adds 12 GenAI risk categories (e.g., confabulation, data privacy, information integrity, IP, information security) that become additional risk-flag fields on the intake form.

**(7) Limitations & failure modes.** MAP is a *framework of outcomes*, not a form — it tells you what to know, not how to ask it; you must author the actual fields. It is also exhaustive: applying all 18 subcategories to a low-risk productivity use case is overkill and breeds intake fatigue. NIST explicitly says the actions "do not constitute a checklist." Mitigation: risk-tier the form so low-risk requests answer a short subset.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Use MAP as the *authoritative source of what to capture* when you need a defensible, audit-ready, regulator-recognized basis for your fields — especially if you may later pursue ISO 42001 certification or face the EU AI Act/Colorado AI Act. It is the spine; the templates below (Trustible, RAI Institute) are faster to operationalize but should be checked back against MAP for completeness.

