## A1·Framework 2 — ISO/IEC 42001:2023 AI System Impact Assessment & Documentation

**(1) Origin.** ISO/IEC 42001:2023, published December 2023 — the world's first certifiable **AI Management System (AIMS)** standard, jointly by ISO and IEC. Certifiable by accredited third-party bodies (unlike NIST AI RMF, which has no certification).

**(2) Mechanism.** ISO 42001 uses the Harmonized Structure shared with ISO 27001/9001: **mandatory Clauses 4–10** (Context, Leadership, Planning, Support, Operation, Performance Evaluation, Improvement) plus **Annex A** (a reference control set) and **Annex B** (implementation guidance). For intake specifically, two things matter: (a) Clause 6.1 requires AI **risk assessment**, and (b) **Annex A control objective A.5** requires an **AI system impact assessment** for systems that affect people. The standard also uniquely requires, as part of scoping the AIMS, that the organization **formally determine its role** (developer, provider, user) toward each in-scope AI system — a field that changes what else you must capture.

**(3) Exact structure.** Annex A contains **38 controls across 9 control objectives (A.2–A.10)**: AI policy; internal organization; resources; **impact assessment (A.5)**; AI system life cycle; data; information for interested parties; responsible use; third-party relationships. Two impact-assessment controls drive intake:
- **A.5.1 (Impact assessment scope)** — conduct an impact assessment for every AI system that affects people (employees, customers, third parties), considering physical, psychological, financial, and discriminatory harms.
- **A.5.2 (Proportionate controls)** — controls implemented must be proportionate to identified impacts; a low-impact, no-sensitive-data system does not need the control intensity of a hiring/credit system.

**(4) Primary-source anchor.** AWS's ISO 42001 guidance notes AI impact assessments (AIIAs) *"are like data protection impact assessments (DPIAs) for high-risk personal data processing"* and recommends they be conducted *"at least annually on existing systems, and prior to the deployment of any new AI function."* That cadence ("prior to deployment of any new function") is an argument for impact-assessment questions living *on the intake form itself*.

**(5) Worked example.** The same vendor-letter assistant: under A.5.1 the impact assessment asks who is affected (buyers, vendors), what harms could arise (a hallucinated commitment in a letter → financial/contractual harm), and under A.5.2 sets proportionate controls (mandatory human sign-off, no autosend) — recorded at intake and carried into the Statement of Applicability.

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** ISO 42001 is purpose-built for AI; its companion **ISO/IEC 42005** (AI system impact assessment) gives the detailed methodology, and **ISO/IEC 23894** gives AI risk-management guidance. Use 42005 to design the impact-assessment section of the intake form.

**(7) Limitations & failure modes.** The controls are "deliberately high-level and principle-based, not prescriptive technical requirements" — they tell you to do an impact assessment but not the exact questions. Certification is effortful; for a Phase-1 internal pilot, adopting the *impact-assessment discipline* without full certification is the pragmatic move.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Choose ISO 42001's impact-assessment structure when certification is a goal, when procurement/customers expect it, or when you want a management-system wrapper (policy → risk → controls → audit) rather than just a framework. It pairs naturally with NIST MAP: MAP frames context, A.5 formalizes the impact assessment, and both feed the same intake form.

