## A1·Framework 3 — Responsible AI Institute "AI Use Case Intake Framework"

**(1) Origin.** Responsible AI Institute (RAI Institute), a non-profit; a published, adaptable intake template designed for cross-functional use.

**(2) Mechanism.** Provides a step-by-step assessment worksheet that walks a proposer and reviewer through use-case description, then risk categorization, then data/privacy evaluation, then accountability assignment. Its distinguishing move is **risk-level categorization explicitly aligned to EU AI Act tiers** (unacceptable / high / limited / minimal), so the intake output is regulator-legible from day one.

**(3) Exact inputs.** Use-case description; intended users and affected parties; EU-AI-Act-aligned risk tier; data sensitivity/privacy evaluation; cross-functional accountability/oversight assignment.

**(5) Worked example.** A customer-facing chatbot routed through this framework lands in the EU AI Act "limited risk / transparency" tier (must disclose it is AI), triggering a transparency-control field; an internal summarizer lands in "minimal," triggering a lighter path.

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** It is AI-native and regulation-forward; its value is mapping a raw idea onto a legal risk tier at intake.

**(7) Limitations.** As a non-profit template it is less granular than a consultancy's bespoke form; treat as a strong starting scaffold.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Best when EU AI Act (or analogous) exposure is real and you want the intake form to pre-classify legal risk.

