## A1·Framework 4 — Trustible Four-Category Intake Model

**(1) Origin.** Trustible (AI governance vendor); a practitioner taxonomy widely circulated via IAPP AI Governance Global presentations (with Leidos and Nuix). It is the most usable distillation of "what belongs on the form."

**(2) Mechanism.** Organizes every field into **four buckets**, governed by one design rule: *every field must map to a question the reviewer actually needs answered*, with the explicit goal of **inter-rater reliability** — "two reviewers evaluating the same submission reach the same decision."

**(3) Exact categories & fields.**
- **Use-Case Basics** — what the AI will do; who is proposing it; which system/vendor is involved.
- **Risk Signals** — personal/sensitive/regulated data involved? consequential decisions about individuals (hiring, lending, healthcare, access)? customer-facing vs internal? jurisdiction(s)?
- **Business Value** — expected outcome; success metrics; estimated investment.
- **Stakeholder Information** — business owner; technical lead; legal/privacy/compliance contacts.

**(5) Worked example.** Vendor-letter assistant: Basics (drafts letters; merchandising proposes; Claude Enterprise). Risk Signals (pricing data = sensitive; no decisions about individuals; internal; US). Value (6 hrs/week; success = adoption + time saved). Stakeholders (buyer lead = owner; IT = technical; Legal = pricing-confidentiality contact).

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** The "Risk Signals" bucket is where you bolt on GenAI-specific flags (hallucination tolerance, training-data provenance, connector/data-access scope).

**(7) Limitations.** Vendor-originated (secondary to NIST/ISO); deliberately lightweight, so high-risk systems still need a deeper impact assessment appended.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Use Trustible's four buckets as the *layout* of your form and NIST MAP/ISO A.5 as the *completeness check* on the fields inside them. This is the recommended default operating combination: Trustible structure, MAP/ISO rigor.

