# LEAF A3 — PRIORITIZATION CRITERIA

**What this leaf must accomplish.** A2 scores candidate *quality*; A3 *ranks the portfolio* so finite pilot capacity goes to the right few. Below, each model gets the full eight-part treatment, followed by a comparison table and the AI-specific overlays.

---

## A3·Framework 1 — RICE

**(1) Origin.** Created by **Sean McBride at Intercom (2018)**, published on the Intercom blog ("RICE: Simple prioritization for product managers"). Built on Intercom's growth team to compare many ideas against a *single* conversion goal — which is why it works cleanest when candidates share a common objective.

**(2) Mechanism.** Split the un-arguable word "impact" into four estimable factors, score each, and combine into one comparable number. Reach, Impact, and Confidence are benefits (numerator); Effort is cost (denominator).

**(3) Exact scales & formula (verbatim from Intercom).**
- **Reach** — how many people/events the initiative affects *in a defined time period* (e.g., users per quarter). Use real analytics, not guesses.
- **Impact** — per-person magnitude on the goal, on a fixed multiple-choice scale: **3 = massive, 2 = high, 1 = medium, 0.5 = low, 0.25 = minimal.** (The fixed scale exists to "temper enthusiasm for exciting but ill-defined ideas.")
- **Confidence** — a percentage reflecting evidence quality: **100% = high (quantitative data), 80% = medium (some data), 50% = low (gut feeling).** Below 50% = "moonshot," deprioritize.
- **Effort** — total team time in **person-months** across product/design/engineering; kept to whole numbers (or 0.5) to avoid false precision.
- **Formula:** **RICE = (Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort.** The result is "total impact per unit of work"; scores are *relative* (a 100 means nothing alone; 100 vs 30 means everything).

**(5) Worked example (AI portfolio).** Candidate: "GenAI onboarding-doc summarizer." Reach = 2,000 employees/quarter hitting onboarding; Impact = 2 (high — measurably faster ramp); Confidence = 80% (some pilot data); Effort = 2 person-months. RICE = (2000 × 2 × 0.8) ÷ 2 = **1,600.** Compare to "AI dark-mode for the portal": Reach 5,000, Impact 0.25, Confidence 100%, Effort 1 → (5000 × 0.25 × 1)/1 = **1,250.** RICE correctly prevents a high-reach "nice-to-have" from outranking a real productivity driver.

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** (a) Tie **Impact** to an AI-relevant outcome (activation, time-saved, error reduction) and define the bands explicitly. (b) **Confidence** is the honest home for AI uncertainty — model reliability, hallucination risk, and unproven workflows all *lower* confidence rather than being ignored. (c) Add a **data-readiness multiplier** (below). (d) Effort must include prompt engineering, evaluation, and change management, not just build.

**(7) Limitations & failure modes.** False precision (multiplying four estimates implies rigor that isn't there). The well-documented gaming pattern: teams inflate Confidence to 80% for pet projects and sandbag it to 50% for rivals — the "Score Integrity Test" catches this. RICE under-weights strategic and time-sensitivity factors and assumes a shared goal (mixed-goal portfolios distort it). NIST-style risk isn't represented unless added.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Choose RICE when you have a portfolio of comparable candidates against a shared objective and want a *ranked list* with explicit uncertainty handling. Prefer it over ICE when Reach varies a lot across candidates (ICE has no Reach term).

---

## A3·Framework 2 — ICE

**(1) Origin.** Popularized by **Sean Ellis** in growth-hacking practice (predates and inspired RICE).

**(2) Mechanism.** Three factors, scored and multiplied — faster and lighter than RICE.

**(3) Exact scale & formula.** **Impact, Confidence, Ease**, each scored **1–10**; **ICE = Impact × Confidence × Ease.** (Some variants score 1–5.)

**(5) Worked example.** "Pricing-page AI copy test": Impact 7, Confidence 6, Ease 9 → ICE = 378. Quick to compute in a whiteboard session.

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** "Ease" naturally captures technical/data simplicity; but because Ease is weighted equally with Impact, ICE *systematically biases toward easy, high-confidence wins* — which in an AI context means it over-ranks shallow copilot tweaks and under-ranks high-value-but-uncertain Extend bets. Counteract by adding a value floor.

**(7) Limitations.** No Reach term (so a tiny-audience easy win can top the list); the easy-win bias above; 1–10 scales are subjective without rubrics.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Use ICE for fast, low-stakes triage or when candidates have similar reach. Graduate to RICE when reach varies or when you need to defend rankings to executives.

---

## A3·Framework 3 — Weighted Scoring Model

**(1) Origin.** Generic multi-criteria decision analysis; standard in product and portfolio management.

**(2) Mechanism.** Choose 4–6 decision criteria, assign each a weight, score every candidate per criterion, and compute a weighted total. Fully customizable — its strength is that it *encodes your specific strategy* into the weights.

**(3) Exact mechanics.** Weights **sum to 100%**; each candidate scored (e.g., 1–5 or 1–10) per criterion; **score = Σ(weight × criterion score).** Example criteria for AI: strategic alignment (25%), value potential (25%), feasibility (15%), data readiness (15%), risk-inverse (10%), reusability (10%).

**(5) Worked example.** Candidate scored 4/5 alignment, 5/5 value, 3/5 feasibility, 2/5 data, 4/5 risk-inverse, 3/5 reuse with the weights above → (0.25·4 + 0.25·5 + 0.15·3 + 0.15·2 + 0.10·4 + 0.10·3) = 3.7/5. The low data score (2) visibly drags the total, which is the point.

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** This is the most natural model for AI because you can make **data readiness** and **risk** first-class weighted criteria rather than bolt-ons.

**(7) Limitations.** Weight-setting is subjective and politically negotiable; criteria can overlap (double-counting); more setup than ICE/RICE.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Choose when strategic priorities must be explicit and auditable, when stakeholders need to *see* their priorities reflected in the weights, or when AI-specific factors (data, risk, reuse) deserve formal weight. Best for a governance committee that must justify its ranking.

---

## A3·Framework 4 — MoSCoW

**(1) Origin.** Dai Clegg (Oracle), 1994; core to DSDM/Agile.

**(2) Mechanism.** Sort items into four commitment categories rather than ranking them numerically.

**(3) Exact categories.** **Must have** (non-negotiable for this release), **Should have** (important, not vital), **Could have** (desirable if capacity allows), **Won't have (this time)** (explicitly deferred).

**(5) Worked example.** Phase-1 AI rollout: *Must* = acceptable-use training + two anchor use cases per function; *Should* = department prompt libraries; *Could* = a comparison-challenge leaderboard; *Won't (now)* = agentic auto-actions.

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** Useful for scoping *what a pilot commits to* and for managing stakeholder expectations about deferred AI capabilities.

**(7) Limitations.** **No ranking within a category** — ten "Must haves" are unordered; "Must" inflation is common. Not a portfolio-value tool.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Use MoSCoW for release/sprint scoping and expectation-setting, layered *on top of* a value-ranking model — not as a substitute for RICE/weighted scoring.

---

## A3·Framework 5 — Value-vs-Feasibility / Impact-vs-Effort 2×2 (Gartner-style)

**(1) Origin.** Classic 2×2 prioritization matrix; Gartner applies a value-vs-feasibility version specifically to AI use-case portfolios.

**(2) Mechanism.** Plot each candidate on two axes — value (vertical) and feasibility/ease (horizontal) — creating four quadrants.

**(3) Exact quadrants.** **Quick Wins** (high value / high feasibility — do first); **Big Bets / Major Projects** (high value / low feasibility — plan deliberately); **Fill-ins / Maybes** (low value / high feasibility — spare capacity); **Money Pits / Time Sinks** (low value / low feasibility — avoid/kill).

**(5) Worked example.** Gartner's 2025 retail assessment evaluated 20 non-generative AI use cases on feasibility and business-value axes; the structured plot *"eliminates low-signal investments before pilot spend is committed."* In a copilot rollout, "summarize internal docs" lands Quick Win; "agentic supply-chain rebalancing" lands Big Bet.

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** Use the feasibility axis to fold in data readiness and model maturity; the matrix is excellent for executive communication of an AI portfolio at a glance.

**(7) Limitations.** Coarse (two dimensions only); placement is judgment-based; hides magnitude differences within a quadrant.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Use as the *visual executive layer* over a numeric model — RICE/weighted scoring decides the ranking; the 2×2 communicates it. Faster than RICE for a quick whiteboard session.

---

## A3·Framework 6 — Gartner AI Project-Prioritization Funnel

**(1) Origin.** Gartner AI strategy guidance.

**(2) Mechanism.** Progressive narrowing — start from 100+ ideas and ruthlessly eliminate at each gate down to 3–5 fundable initiatives, applying the Defend/Extend/Upend lens and value/feasibility scoring at each stage.

**(3) Exact logic.** Funnel stages cull on successively harder criteria (strategic fit → value → feasibility → risk/cost). Gartner's caution underscores the discipline: per its June 2025 press release, *"over 40% of agentic AI projects will be canceled by the end of 2027, due to escalating costs, unclear business value or inadequate risk controls."*

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** Built for AI; especially relevant given "agent washing" — Gartner estimates only ~130 of thousands of self-described agentic-AI vendors are genuine, so the funnel's later gates should test vendor/technical reality.

**(7) Limitations.** Requires enough idea volume to be worth funneling; gate criteria must be pre-agreed or the culling becomes political.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Use when you have *many* candidate ideas and need a defensible whittling process feeding a stage-gate (A4).

---

## A3·Framework 7 — Kano Model

**(1) Origin.** Noriaki Kano, 1984.

**(2) Mechanism.** Classifies *features* by the type of customer satisfaction they produce, via paired functional/dysfunctional survey questions.

**(3) Exact categories.** **Basic/Must-be** (absence causes dissatisfaction, presence is expected), **Performance/One-dimensional** (satisfaction scales linearly with the feature), **Excitement/Delighter** (unexpected, disproportionately delights), plus Indifferent and Reverse.

**(5) Worked example.** For a member-facing AI assistant: accurate answers = Basic; faster resolution = Performance; proactive personalized suggestions = Delighter.

**(6) AI-specific adaptation.** Relevant when prioritizing *user-facing AI features*, less so for internal-productivity portfolios.

**(7) Limitations.** Survey-dependent; classifications drift over time (today's delighter becomes tomorrow's basic — true of most AI features); not a portfolio-ranking tool.

**(8) Selection guidance.** Use alongside RICE when the candidates are customer-facing AI *features* whose value depends on satisfaction type, not just magnitude.

---

## A3·AI-Specific Overlays (layer onto any model above)

- **Data-availability gate** — a hard minimum data-readiness score; below it, the candidate cannot advance regardless of value. (Addresses the #1 scaling failure.)
- **Model-risk multiplier** — discount the score for candidates with high hallucination/consequence risk (e.g., multiply by 0.7 for high model risk), forcing higher value to compensate.
- **Regulatory-exposure factor** — down-rank or route-to-deeper-review candidates touching consequential decisions about people or customer-facing disclosure duties.
- **Reusability/leverage bonus** — up-rank candidates whose prompts/patterns generalize across functions (a force-multiplier in an enablement program).

| Model | Formula / mechanism | Dimensions | Strength | Weakness | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **RICE** | (R×I×C)/E | Reach, Impact, Confidence, Effort | Objective, ranked, handles uncertainty | False precision; gameable; assumes shared goal | Comparable portfolio vs one goal |
| **ICE** | I×C×E | Impact, Confidence, Ease | Fast, light | No reach; easy-win bias | Quick low-stakes triage |
| **Weighted Scoring** | Σ(weight×score) | Custom 4–6 | Encodes strategy; AI factors first-class | Subjective weights; overlap | Auditable committee ranking |
| **MoSCoW** | Categorical | Must/Should/Could/Won't | Simple; expectation-setting | No intra-rank; "Must" inflation | Release scoping |
| **Value×Feasibility 2×2** | Quadrant plot | Value, Feasibility | Visual; executive-friendly | Coarse; hides magnitude | Communicating a portfolio |
| **Gartner Funnel** | Progressive culling | Multi-gate | Handles many ideas | Needs idea volume | Whittling 100→5 |
| **Kano** | Satisfaction typing | Basic/Perf/Delight | Reveals feature *type* | Survey-bound; drifts | User-facing AI features |

