## A4·Decision-Rights Frameworks — RACI vs RAPID vs DACI (compared in depth)

Gates need unambiguous decision rights, or they stall. Three models dominate; they are *not* interchangeable.

### RACI (task-centric)
- **(1) Origin.** Long-standing project-management responsibility-assignment matrix.
- **(2)(3) Roles.** **Responsible** (does the work — can be several), **Accountable** (owns the outcome — *exactly one* per task, the "one-accountable rule"), **Consulted** (two-way input before action), **Informed** (one-way, kept in the loop).
- **(5) AI application.** Best for **ongoing operational responsibilities** — e.g., who maintains the prompt library (R), who owns adoption metrics (A), who is consulted on data classification (C), who is informed of incidents (I).
- **(7) Limitation.** It assigns *task* responsibility, not *decision* authority; it doesn't model who can veto a decision. Over-use produces "everyone Consulted" sludge.
- **(8) Use when.** Mapping who-does-what across a workstream, not who-decides at a gate.

### RAPID® (decision-centric, with explicit veto)
- **(1) Origin.** **Bain & Company**; popularized by Paul Rogers & Marcia Blenko in the *Harvard Business Review* article **"Who Has the D?"** (2006).
- **(2)(3) Roles** (the letters are a loose acronym, not a sequence): **Recommend** (drives the proposal and analysis), **Agree** (must formally sign off — *an explicit veto right*), **Perform** (executes once decided), **Input** (consulted for facts/feasibility, non-binding), **Decide** (the single decision-maker — "the D").
- **(5) AI application.** Best for **high-stakes gate decisions**. Example for "approve customer-facing GenAI to pilot": Recommend = AI enablement lead; **Agree = Legal + Security** (real veto on a customer-facing system); Input = affected business unit; Decide = the AI governance board chair/executive; Perform = the delivery team.
- **(7) Limitation.** Heavier to set up; the explicit "Agree" veto can slow decisions (which is sometimes correct).
- **(8) Use when.** Multi-stakeholder, irreversible, or risk-laden decisions where second-line functions (legal/risk/security) genuinely hold a veto. The **"Agree" role maps precisely onto AI second-line controls**, which is why RAPID fits AI gates better than RACI.

### DACI (decision-centric, no explicit veto)
- **(1) Origin.** Popularized by Intuit/Atlassian.
- **(2)(3) Roles.** **Driver** (moves the decision forward), **Approver** (makes the final call — usually one), **Contributors** (provide expertise/input), **Informed** (notified of the outcome).
- **(5) AI application.** A lighter middle ground for routine gate decisions where you want a clear single Approver without formalizing vetoes — e.g., approving low-risk internal use cases to pilot.
- **(7) Limitation.** No explicit veto means high-risk decisions may under-weight legal/security objections; less suited to consequential customer-facing AI.
- **(8) Use when.** You need decisive single-approver clarity with speed, for low/medium-risk decisions.

| Model | Centric on | Single owner role | Explicit veto? | Best AI use |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **RACI** | Tasks | Accountable (1) | No | Ongoing operational ownership |
| **RAPID** | Decisions | Decide (1) | **Yes — "Agree"** | High-stakes / customer-facing gates |
| **DACI** | Decisions | Approver (1) | No | Fast low/medium-risk gate decisions |

**Recommended pattern:** **RAPID (or DACI) for gate decisions, RACI for operational responsibilities** — they are complementary, not competing.

